   "text": "## Summary\n\nI searched the feature space systematically:\n\n1. **Single-feature scan**: Evaluated all 40 features individually to identify the strongest standalone signals. Top performers included feature 4 (+0.388), feature 13 (-0.336, i.e., short), feature 15 (-0.299, short), feature 21 (+0.266), and feature 25 (+0.242).\n\n2. **Pairwise combination**: Combined the top single features, finding that features 4 (long) and 13 (short) together produced a strong Sharpe of 0.509 \u2014 better than either alone, suggesting complementary/diversifying signal.\n\n3. **Triplet search**: Extended the best pairs by adding a third feature. The combination of features **[4, 13, 15]** with signs **[+1, -1, -1]** produced the best in-sample Sharpe of **0.592**, and this was robust \u2014 verified via repeated evaluation and confirmed as a local optimum against many alternative third-feature substitutions and sign flips.\n\n4. **Final specification submitted**: features `[4, 13, 15]`, signs `[1, -1, -1]`, in-sample Sharpe 0.592.\n\n**Predicted OOS belief**: mean Sharpe 0.35, sd 0.25. I shaded meaningfully below the in-sample value of 0.592 because:\n- The spec was chosen via a search over ~70 evaluations across 40 features, introducing selection/overfitting bias.\n- Individual feature signals were modest in magnitude (~0.1\u20130.4), so the combined result partly reflects genuine diversification but also some in-sample luck.\n- A wide standard deviation (0.25) reflects substantial uncertainty about how much of the in-sample edge will persist out-of-sample."
